## Authored content — this case HAS some, and it is quoted

  `tools/tb tool tb277 ckt1406c ckcase AWPTCM-T43864` -> ck.db `zephyr_cases` id 40184, key
  AWPTCM-T43864, title "(41) IPv4_MulticastRouting - PIM-DM",
  folder /New Platform Test (MASTER)/New Platform Template/IPv4, priority **High**,
  status Draft, `num_steps` 1.  Verbatim:

    objective:    "PIM-DM works as expected DUT can route multicast traffic using PIM-DM"
    precondition: "IXIA -- DUT -- sw1(running PIM-DM)-- IXIA"
    steps_text:   "Setup a PIM-DM network  Multicast client able to get multicast stream"
    expected:     (the single step carries no separate expected-result text)

  So there IS an authored objective and an authored TOPOLOGY, but no step-by-step procedure and
  no authored expected result.  **The authored topology is satisfied exactly**: the group-C
  two-router bench is `IXIA -- DUT -- sw1 -- IXIA` with the two host NICs standing in for the
  two IXIA ports and **swi_a as `sw1`, genuinely running PIM-DM** (`service pdm`,
  `ip pim dense-mode`, adjacency up — proofs below).  The rest of the procedure below is
  DESIGNED, from RFC 3973 and the DUT's CLI surface, and is labelled as such.

  Designed limbs and why each is here: PIM-DM is *defined* by flood-and-prune plus Graft, so a
  PIM-DM verdict that only showed "a joined client gets the stream" would not distinguish it
  from PIM-SM at all.  The limbs below therefore include the three things that are PIM-DM and
  not PIM-SM: the prune of a branch with no receiver, the **State Refresh** that keeps that
  prune alive, and the **Graft** that reverses it.

## Topology and the service gate

  Group C's shared two-router topology, unchanged from T43863 (diagram and the NEW-RISK-8 STP
  remedy are in T43863's log; re-confirmed here after each reload).  PIM-SM was fully removed at
  the end of T43863, so this case began from a multicast-OFF bench.

    swi_d(config)#service pdm           accepted, 17:01 (no error)
    swi_a(config)#service pdm           accepted, 17:01 (no error)
    awplus#show process | include pdm
    3343 pdmd                5   0.0   0.1    20 sleep                   <- the daemon IS running

  🔴 **swi_b cannot be `sw1`**: `service pdm` on swi_b answers
  `% Service is not currently licensed on this platform` (finding O-20, measured at group
  setup).  That is why `sw1` is swi_a.

  🔴 **F-13 applied up front, and then it bit anyway — which confirms it.**  T43863 established
  that `ip pim <mode>` does not enable router-side IGMP.  `ip igmp` was therefore included in
  this case's setup.  But the baseline join at 17:01:45 was sent BEFORE that line was applied,
  so it landed in the snooping table only, and the first PIM-DM forwarding leg read 0/10 for a
  group that looked joined.  Re-joining AFTER `ip igmp` was enabled gave 10/10 with nothing else
  changed (legs 3a/3b).  **A membership learned while the interface was snoop-only does not
  migrate when router-side IGMP is later enabled — it must be re-sent.**  Carried into F-13.

    swi_a#show ip igmp interface vlan48
     IGMP Enabled, Active, Querier, Version 3 (default)
    swi_a#show ip pim dense-mode neighbor      (read on the DUT, same adjacency)
    Neighbor-Address Interface          Uptime/Expires    Ver
    10.0.61.2        vlan61             00:00:04/00:01:43 v2
    awplus#show ip pim dense-mode interface
    Address          Interface VIFIndex Ver/   Nbr
    10.38.22.34      vlan10    3        v2/D   0           <- D = DENSE mode
    10.0.61.1        vlan61    5        v2/D   1

## Measurement stance

  As T43863: `tools/arpleg.py` legs from host eth3 counted on host eth4, each carrying a
  never-joined control group and an **instrument control** (routed ICMP 10.38.22.40 ->
  10.38.22.65) that must arrive.  The instrument control arrived at FULL count in all 11
  counted legs of this case, so every zero here is a measurement.  Control-plane claims are
  decoded from the DUT's own `tcpdump` on vlan 61, the routed-only transit segment.
  Group addresses are all distinct in their low 23 bits, so no two share a link-layer address.

